==English==

===Etymology===
An [[allusion]] to a situation in which a hunting dog misidentifies the tree up which it has chased an animal and positions itself at the base of another tree, barking upward at the branches.

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|en-au-bark up the wrong tree.ogg|Audio (AU)}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|*}}

# {{lb|en|idiomatic}} To [[do]], [[believe]], or [[pursue]] something [[wrong]] or [[inappropriate]]; to take the wrong [[approach]]; to follow a [[false]] [[lead]]; to [[blame]] or [[rebuke]] the wrong person.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1894|author={{w|Robert Barr}}|title=In the Midst of Alarms|chapter=21|passage=You're not the first man who has made such a mistake, and found he was '''barking up the wrong tree'''.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1915|author={{w|John Buchan}}|title=The Thirty-Nine Steps|chapter=10|passage=They all went into the house, and left me feeling a precious idiot. I had been '''barking up the wrong tree''' this time.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1922|author={{w|William MacLeod Raine}}|title=Man Size|chapter=19|passage="We want West. He's a cowardly murderer—killed the man who trusted him." . . .}}
#*: "Of course we may be '''barking up the wrong tree'''," the officer reflected aloud. "Maybe West isn't within five hundred miles of here."
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=September 2 2008|author=[[w:Ken Russell|Ken Russell]]|title=Let my life flash before you, in paperback|titleurl=http://entertainment.timesonline.co.uk/tol/arts_and_entertainment/film/article4654486.ece|journal=Times Online|accessdate=1 Oct 2010|location=UK
|passage=After three failed marriages I realised that I may have been '''barking up the wrong tree''' and should abandon the search for the perfect wife.}}

====Usage notes====
* Often used in the [[present participle|present participial]] form: ''[[barking up the wrong tree]]''.

